==Old English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|ang|id=plural|:der<unc>|gem-pro:*-anz|gem-pro:*-ōs<ety:from<gem-pro:*-ōz<id:accusative plural>>>}}
Perhaps from the Proto-Germanic accusative plural ending *''-anz'', with regularly lost ''-n-'' before a fricative, or perhaps from the nominative plural *''-ōs'', a voiceless variety of the regular ending *''-ōz'', or a merger of both. Akin to {{cog|stq|-s}}, {{cog|fy|-s}}, {{cog|osx|-os}} ({{cog|nds|-s}}), {{cog|nl|-s}}, {{cog|sv|-ar}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ang-IPA|-as}}

====Suffix====
{{h|ang|suf}}

# {{non-gloss|Plural ending of nominative and accusative cases, originally of a-stem masculine nouns, later extended to other nouns.}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|hund|gloss1=dog|hundas|gloss2=dogs}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|wulf|gloss1=wolf|wulfas|gloss2=wolves}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desctree|enm|-es|id=plural}}

===Etymology 2===

====Suffix====
{{h|ang|suf}} {{tlb|ang|Anglian}}

# {{alternative form of|ang|-ast}}; {{n-g|marks the second person singular present indicative of class II weak verbs}}

