Page 26 -e: -------- begin text --------
==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|-e}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|ang|id=adverbial|:inh|gmw-pro:<alt:*-ā><ety:inh<gem-pro:*-ê>>}}
From West Germanic *''-ā'', from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ê}}, which survives otherwise only in Gothic (and possibly Old Norse).

====Suffix====
{{ang-adv}}

# {{senseid|ang|adverbial}} {{n-g|forms adverbs from adjectives}}; [[-ly]]
#: {{sufex|ang|beorht|t1=bright, clear|beorhte|t2=brightly, clearly}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|ang|-a|-æ||Northumbrian}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|ang|id=adverbial}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|enm|-e|id=adverbial|qq=no longer productive}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-ī|id=adjective}}. Beyond that, generally from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ijaz}}, but also partly merged with {{m|gem-pro|*-jaz}} and athematic {{m|gem-pro|*-iz|id=adjective}} (compare {{cog|gmq-pro|-ᛁᛉ}}). Note though that {{m|ang|-wintre}} is from {{ncog|gem-pro|*-wintruz}}, since Proto-Germanic ''u''-stem adjectives became ''ja''-stems in West Germanic. Other suffixes derived from u-stem nouns, such as {{m|ang|-flēre}}, might also have been [[w:conversion (word formation)|zero derivations]] in Proto-Germanic.

====Suffix====
{{ang-adj}}

# {{senseid|ang|adjectival}} {{ng|forms adjectives from nouns, often with the meaning "having"; typically causes i-umlaut}}
#: {{sufex|ang|ān|t1=one|willa|t2=will|ānwille|t3=stubborn}}
#: {{sufex|ang|fela|t1=many|word|t2=word|felawyrde|t3=long-winded}}
#: {{sufex|ang|fīf|t1=five|flōr|t2=floor|fīfflēre|t3=five-story}}
#: {{sufex|ang|fiþer-|t1=four|sċēat|t2=corner|fiþersċīte|t3=square}}
#: {{sufex|ang|forþ|t1=forward|gang|t2=going|forþgenġe|t3=progressive}}
#: {{sufex|ang|īdel|t1=empty|hand|t2=hand|īdelhende|t3=empty-handed}}
#: {{sufex|ang|lang|t1=long|līf|t2=life|langlīfe|t3=long-lived}}
#: {{sufex|ang|ofer-|t1=over-|ǣt|t2=eating|oferǣte|t3=gluttonous}}
#: {{sufex|ang|or-|t1=out of|blōd|t2=blood|orblēde|t3=bled out}}
#: {{sufex|ang|sċeolh|t1=crooked|ēage|t2=eye|sċeolhīeġe|t3=crosseyed}}
#: {{sufex|ang|sīd|t1=hanging down|feax|t2=hair on the head|sīdfiexe|t3=long-haired}}
#: {{sufex|ang|twelf|t1=twelve|winter|t2=year|twelfwintre|t3=twelve years old}}
#: {{sufex|ang|twi-|t1=two|eċġ|t2=edge|twieċġe|t3=double-edged}}
#: {{sufex|ang|twi-|t1=two|fōt|t2=foot|twifēte|t3=bipedal}}
#: {{sufex|ang|un-|t1=un-|ċēap|t2=cost|unċīepe|t3=free}}

=====Declension=====
{{ang-adecl|-e|short=1}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|ang|-i||Early Anglian}}

====See also====
* {{suffixsee|ang|id=adjectival}}

===Etymology 3===
Likely borrowed from the subjunctive singular {{der|ang|gmw-pro|*-ē}} after high vowel [[apocope]] resulted in the loss of inherited {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-u}} in heavy stem verbs, and then analogically extended to light stem verbs.

====Suffix====
{{ang-head|verbf}}

# {{ng|marks the {{lg|first person}} {{lg|singular}} {{lg|present}} {{lg|indicative}} of {{lg|strong verb}}s and class I {{lg|weak verb}}s}}
#: {{sufex|ang|drīfan|t1=to drive|drīfe|t2=I drive}}
#: {{sufex|ang|fremman|t1=to perform|fremme|t2=I perform}}

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|ang|-u|-o||Anglian|Kentish}}

===Etymology 4===
{{etymid|ang|dative}}
{{inh+|ang|gmw-pro|*-ē|id=dative}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ai
|pos=''a''-stem}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-ōi|pos=''ō''-stem}}, from the {{der|ang|ine-pro|-}} dative singular ending {{m|ine-pro|*-ey}} or locative singular ending {{m|ine-pro|*-i}} as attached to ''eh₂''-stem and ''o''-stem nouns.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ang|-æ||early}}

====Suffix====
{{ang-head|nounf|g=m,f,n}}

# {{ng|marks the {{lg|dative}} {{lg|singular}} of ''a''-stem and ''ō''-stem nouns}}
#: {{sufex|ang|sċip|t1=ship|[[tō]] [[þām]] [[scipe|sċip'''e''']]|t2=to the ship}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|enm|-e|id=dative}}

===Etymology 5===
{{etymid|ang|ō-stem}}
A combination of multiple sources:
* In the accusative singular, from {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-ā|id=singular}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ǭ|id=accusative singular}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*-ā́m}}, from {{af|ine-pro|*-eh₂|*-m|pos1=''eh₂''-stem suffix|pos2=accusative singular suffix|nocat=1}}.
* In the genitive singular, from {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-ā|id=singular}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ōz|id=genitive singular}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*-eh₂s}}, from {{af|ine-pro|*-eh₂|*-s|pos1=''eh₂''-stem suffix|pos2=genitive singular suffix|nocat=1}}.
* In the accusative plural, from {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-ā|id=ō-stem accusative plural}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ōz|id=accusative plural}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*-eh₂m̥s}}, from {{af|ine-pro|*-eh₂|*-ms|pos1=''eh₂''-stem suffix|pos2=accusative plural suffix|nocat=1}}.
This suffix was extended to the nominative plural (replacing {{m|ang|-a}} < {{m+|gmw-pro|*-ō|id=ō-stem nominative plural}}) in Old English itself.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ang|-æ||early}}

====Suffix====
{{ang-head|nounf|g=f}}

# {{ng|marks the {{lg|accusative}} and {{lg|genitive}} and the {{lg|nominative}} and accusative {{lg|plural}} of ō-stem feminine nouns}}
#: {{sufex|ang|talu|t1=tale|tale|t2=tale, tales}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The names for several peoples are formed this way, such as {{m|ang|Dene||Danes}} and {{m|ang|Ēste||Estonians}}.

===Etymology 6===
{{etymid|ang|n-stem}}

====Suffix====
{{ang-head|nounf|g=f}}

# {{ng|marks the nominative singular of feminine n-stem nouns}}
#: {{m|ang|docce|t=dock}}
#: {{m|ang|berġe|t=berry}}

====Suffix====
{{ang-noun||f}}

# {{senseid|ang|feminine}} {{ng|forms feminine agent nouns, often as a counterpart to masculine agent nouns in {{l|ang|-a}}}}
#: {{usex|ang|[[hlǣfdīġe|hlǣfdīġ'''e''']]|t=bread-kneader, lady}}
#: {{sufex|ang|ġebedda|t1=husband, male consort|ġebedde|t2=wife, female consort}}
# {{senseid|ang|feminine}} {{ng|forms nouns referring to female people from nouns referring to male people}}
#: {{sufex|ang|cyning|t1=king|cyninge|t2=queen}}

=====Declension=====
{{ang-decl-noun-n-f|-}}

=====See also=====
* {{suffixsee|ang|id=feminine}}

===Etymology 7===
Likely derived from the weakening of the plural subjunctive ending {{m|ang|-en}}.

====Suffix====
{{ang-head|verbf}}

# {{senseid|ang|second person plural imperative}} {{lb|ang|used with postpositioned {{l|ang|ġē|"ġē"}}}} {{ng|marks the second person plural imperative of strong verbs, class 1 weak verbs, and class 3 weak verbs}}
#: {{usex|ang|Ġebīd'''e''' ġē on beorge!|t=Tarry ye on the mountain!}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|ang|-iġe|-ie|id=second person plural imperative}}

