Page 50 -enne: -------- begin text --------
==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|-anne|-onne}}
* {{alter|ang|-ienne|-ianne}} {{q|used with Class II weak verbs}}
* {{alter|ang|-annae||early <<Mercian>>}}; {{alter|ang|-ene|-ane||late Old English}}

===Etymology===
{{inh+|ang|gmw-pro|*-annjē}}, dative inflection of the infinitive {{m|gmw-pro|-an}}; equivalent to {{m|ang|-an}} + {{af|ang|-e}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|enne}}

===Suffix===
{{h|ang|suf}}

# {{n-g|Forms the inflected infinitive of verbs}}.
#: {{ux|ang|[[to]] [[farenne|far'''enne''']]|to go'''{{l|mul|-∅}}'''|ref=&nbsp;<small>[Mt. Bos. 8, 21]</small>}}

====Usage notes====
* The inflected infinitive is preceded by the preposition {{m|ang|tō}}.
* On contracted verbs, this ending reduced to just ''-ne'': {{m|ang|fōnne}}, {{m|ang|hōnne}}, {{m|ang|tēonne}}.
* The form {{m|ang|-anne}} was very common; this is traditionally taken as influence from the uninflected infinitive {{m|ang|-an}}, but Versloot suggests that since {{m|ang|-anne}} was attested earlier, it must have been original and ''i''-umlaut therefore did not apply in the context of {{m|gmw-pro|*-annjē}}. It is also possible that ''-enne'' reflects a reduction of {{m|ang|-anne}}, but {{m|ang|-anne}}, {{m|ang|-annae}} is in turn a levelling of a prehistoric {{m|ang||*-ennǣ}}.
* This suffix was used with Class I weak verbs and strong verbs; Class II weak verbs used the related {{m|ang|-ienne}} and {{m|ang|-ianne}}.

====Descendants====
* {{desc|enm|-ene|-ende|qq=mostly early|id1=inflected infinitive}}

====See also====
{{ang-conj|-an<w1/ge:0>}}

===Further reading===
* {{cite-book|author={{w|Joseph Bosworth|Bosworth, Joseph}}|editor={{w|Thomas Northcote Toller}}|title={{w|An Anglo-Saxon Dictionary}}|year=1898|location={{w|Oxford}}|publisher=[[w:Oxford University Press|Clarendon Press]]|section=<abbr title="sub verbo">''s.v.''</abbr> “-enne”|page=252/1|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/anglosaxondictio00bosw/page/252/mode/1up}}
* {{cite-journal|date=18 April 2024|last=Versloot|first=Arjen P.|title=The Old English gerund in ''‑enne'' or ''‑anne''
|journal=North-Western European Language Evolution (NOWELE)|volume=77|publisher=w:John Benjamins Publishing Company|doi=https://doi.org/10.1075/nowele.00082.ver|issn=2212-9715|oclc=10224845762|pages=14-22}}
-------- end text --------
