===Suffix===
{{head|ang|suffix|head=-līċ|comparative|-līcra|superlative|-līcost}}

# ''adjective-forming suffix meaning 'like' or 'relating to'''
#: {{suffixusex|ang|ċild|gloss1=child|ċildlīċ|gloss2=childlike}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|dēad|gloss1=dead|dēadlīċ|gloss2=mortal}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|fēond|gloss1=enemy|fēondlīċ|gloss2=hostile}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|frēond|gloss1=friend|frēondlīċ|gloss2=friendly}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|ġefēra|gloss1=companion|ġefērlīċ|gloss2=social}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|lēof|gloss1=beloved|lēoflīċ|gloss2=lovely}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|lēoþ|gloss1=poem|lēoþlīċ|gloss2=poetic}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|līchama|gloss1=body|līchamlīċ|gloss2=physical}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|mennisċ|gloss1=human|mennisċlīċ|gloss2=humane}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|niht|gloss1=night|nihtlīċ|gloss2=nocturnal}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|niþer|gloss1=down|niþerlīċ|gloss2=low}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|open|gloss1=open|openlīċ|gloss2=public}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|stæf|gloss1=letter|stæflīċ|gloss2=literal}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|stōw|gloss1=place|stōwlīċ|gloss2=local}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|ūþwita|gloss1=philosopher|ūþwitlīċ|gloss2=philosophical}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|wer|gloss1=man|werlīċ|gloss2=manly or masculine}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|wīf|gloss1=woman|wīflīċ|gloss2=feminine}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|wundor|gloss1=miracle or wonder|wundorlīċ|gloss2=miraculous or wonderful}}
# ''denoting multiplicity'': [[-fold]]
#: {{uxi|ang|[[þrilīċ]]|triple, three-fold}}

====Usage notes====
* ''-Līċ'' is the most common adjectival suffix in Old English. It can be added to almost any word, often corresponding to Modern English {{m|en|-y}}.

* It has no very definite difference in meaning from {{m|ang|-isċ}} and {{m|ang|-iġ}}, except that it is more common and different words happen to end in it.

* ''-Līċ'' belongs to a class of suffixes which were derived from independent words, but which by the written period were no longer strongly identified with the words they were derived from (in this case, {{m|ang|līċ}} “dead body,” which once meant “body” ← “form”). According to Hogg,<ref>{{R:ang:GOE|volume=1|49}}</ref> the primary examples are {{m|ang|-bǣre}}, {{m|ang|-bora}}, {{m|ang|-cund}}, {{m|ang|-dōm}}, {{m|ang|-fæst}}, {{m|ang|-feald}}, {{m|ang|-ful}}, {{m|ang|-hād}}, {{m|ang|-lāc}}, {{m|ang|-lēas}}, ''-līċ'', {{m|ang|-rǣden}}, {{m|ang|-sċipe}}, {{m|ang|-sum}}, and {{m|ang|-weard}}.

* The words created with these suffixes are called "quasi-compounds," which are stressed differently from true compounds. True compound nouns and adjectives always carry {{w|secondary stress}} on their final element: {{m|ang|foxhol}}, [ˈfoksˌhol]. But quasi-compounds are stressed variably: after a stressed syllable, i.e. a monosyllabic first element, the suffix undergoes reduced stress, unless the suffix is multisyllabic and not {{w|Resolution (meter)|resolved}}. Under this reduced stress, if the suffix has a long vowel or a diphthong, the long vowel is shortened and the diphthong becomes a monophthong (''ea'' becomes ''a'', ''eo'' becomes ''e'', ''ie'' becomes ''i'').

* However, even when the vowel in these suffixes is short, it is traditionally written with a {{w|macron}} for consistency, because it is still long in the inflected forms. Likewise, the quasi-compounding suffixes {{m|ang|-feald}} and {{m|ang|-weard}} are often written with a diphthong even when they would have been pronounced ''-fald'', ''-ward''. Note that after an unstressed syllable, i.e. when the word it suffixes is multisyllabic, the long vowel/diphthong is retained even absent inflectional endings: {{m|ang|brōþorlīċ||brotherly}}, {{m|ang|mæġdenlīċ||girlish}}.

* Note that personal names are stressed exactly the same way, with alterations such as ''Ælfred'' ("Alfred"), ''Ælfrēdes'' ("Alfred's") and ''Ēadward'', ''Ēadweardes''.

* Throughout Old English, the palatal stops [t͡ʃ] and [d͡ʒ] were depalatalized directly before a consonant: ''-līcne'' (acc. sg. masc.), ''līcra'' (gen. pl.). This rule can also be seen in many other words, such as {{m|ang|þenċan}}, 2,3sg. pr. ind. ''þencst'', ''þencþ'', whence Modern English {{m|en|think}}. Note that the palatal fricatives [ʃ] and [j] remained palatal in this condition.<ref>{{R:ang:DOE|page=279}}</ref>

* [[w:Phonological history of Old English#Palatalization|Palatalization]] never occurred directly before a back vowel (a, o, u) except to initial /sk/, so the c is pronounced like a hard k in the inflections ''-līcum'', ''-līca'', and ''-līcan''; also nom. sg. fem./nom-acc. pl. neut. ''-līc(u)'', which always ended in ''*-u'' at the time of palatalization. This same rule also produced many other alterations such as {{m|ang|fisċ||fish}}, pl. ''fiscas'' (later also ''fixas'' by {{w|metathesis}}); {{m|ang|wīċ||village}}, pl. ''wīc'' (whence Modern English {{m|en|-wich}} and {{m|en|-wick}} by [[w:Morphological leveling|leveling]]); and {{m|ang|ġelīċ||like}}, dat. pl. ''ġelīcum'', nom. sg. fem./nom-acc. pl. neut. ''ġelīc'', nom. pl. fem. ''ġelīca'', etc., whence Modern English {{m|en|like}}. So for most words ending in ġ, ċ, or sċ; note however that a few words once featured a following ''*(i)j'' which blocked a back vowel from preventing palatalization and later vanished, as in {{m|ang|rīċe||kingdom}}, pl. {{m|ang|rīċu}} ← ''*rīkju''.<ref>{{R:ang:GOE|volume=1|253}}</ref> Also, historical i-stems began to take non-i-stem endings only after palatalization had run its course: {{m|ang|swēġ||sound}}, pl. ''swēġas'' ← ''*swōġī''. Finally, [j] from original /j/ (as opposed to /ɣ/) was never depalatalized: {{m|ang|twēoġan||to doubt}} ← ''*twihōjan''.

