Page 174 -oþ: Skipped, no changes
Page 175 -oþa: -------- begin text --------
==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|-oþa}}

===Etymology 1===
Perhaps from a {{inh|ang|gmw-pro||*tegunþ-|t=decad|pos=akin to {{m|gmw-pro|*tehun}} "ten"}} in phrases such as {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|alt={{l|gmw-pro|*þriddjō}} tegunþ-|t=the third decad}} which came to be used as ordinals, leading the initial ordinal + ''*-teg-'' to be reanalysed as a cardinal number suffixed with {{m|gmw-pro||*tigiwi}} (> {{m+|ang|-tiġ}}) and the remaining ''-anþ''/''-unþ'' as an ordinal suffix. This was then supplied  with ''*-ō'' (> {{m+|ang||-a}}) like the other ordinals, resulting in a ''-anþō'' or ''-unþō'' that regularly develops to {{m|ang||-oþa}}.<ref>{{R:gem:Bammesberger:1986|pages=5-7}}</ref><ref>{{R:ine:Gvozdanović:1992|15|637}}</ref>

Alternaitvely, a later analogical formation from {{m|ang|seofoþa|t=seventh}}, {{m|ang|eahtoþa|t=eighth}}, {{m|ang|nigoþa|t=ninth}}, and {{m|ang|teogoþa}}, alternative form of {{m|ang|tēoþa|t=tenth}}, with the ''-o-'' of the root reanalysed as belonging to the suffix (etymologically {{m|ang|-da}}, {{m|ang|-ta}}, {{m|ang|-þa}}).<ref>{{R:gem:CGEGL|10|9|235}}</ref>

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix}}

# {{n-g|Forms ordinal numbers from {{m|ang|twēntigoþa|t=20th}} to {{m|ang|hundendleftigoþa
|t=110th}} (and possibly unattested {{m|ang||*hundtwelftigoþa|t=120th}})}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|ang}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desctree|enm|-the|id=ordinal|qq=conflated with {{m|ang|-þa}}}}

=====References=====
{{reflist}}

===Etymology 2===

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix form}}

# {{infl of|ang|-oþ||gen|pl}}
-------- end text --------
