Page 211 -u: -------- begin text --------
==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|-o}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|-u}}

===Etymology 1===
From suppletive fusion of ō-stem feminine {{lg|singular}} {{lg|nominative}} ending {{m|ang|-u}} and {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-į̄||feminine abstract ending}}. Akin to [[w:Gothic language|Gothic]] feminine abstracts in {{m|got|-𐌴𐌹}} (compare {{m|got|𐌼𐌹𐌺𐌹𐌻𐌴𐌹||greatness}}; {{m|got|𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍀𐌴𐌹||depth}}).

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix|head=-u|g=f}}

# {{n-g|ending used to form abstract nouns from adjectives (compare Modern English {{m|en|-ness}}), normally causing i-mutation, and remaining even when preceded by a long syllable}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|eald|gloss1=old|ieldu|gloss2=age}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|hāliġ|gloss1=holy, sacred; pious|hāliġu|gloss2=holiness}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|hāl|gloss1=sound, healthy, intact|hǣlu|gloss2=wholeness, health}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|hāt|gloss1=hot|hǣtu|gloss2=heat, warmth}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|mennisċ|gloss1=human, natural, humane|mennisċu|gloss2=humanity}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|miċel|gloss1=big, large; great|miċelu|gloss2=greatness, size}}

=====Usage notes=====
According to Ringe and Taylor[https://academic.oup.com/book/36266], this suffix began as indeclinable ''*-i'' in the singular and nominative/accusative plural, before the ō-stem feminine nominative singular was borrowed. In Anglian, the new ending was then extended to the rest of these forms. This same extension was not complete in Early West Saxon, but eventually won out in Late West Saxon. Spellings of these forms with ''-o'', and rarely ''-a'', reflect the merger of unstressed back vowels in later Old English.

=====Declension=====
{{ang-decl-noun-in-f|-}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|ang}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-u}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ō}}. [[apocope|Apocope]] should have caused the loss of this ending after heavy stems, but the Anglian dialects generally retained it by analogy with short stems, or for its morphological significance. In West Saxon, this apocope led to replacement with the {{lg|subjunctive}} {{lg|singular}} {{m|ang|-e}}, which was then extended to short stem verbs as well.

====Suffix====
{{h|ang|suf}} {{tlb|ang|Anglian}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the {{lg|first person}} singular {{lg|present}} {{lg|indicative}} of {{lg|strong verb}}s and class I {{lg|weak verb}}s}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|beran|gloss1=bear|beru|t2=I bear}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|drīfan|gloss1=drive out|drīfu|t2=I drive out}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|sellan|gloss1=give|sellu|t2=I give}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-u}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ō}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix|head=-u|g=f}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the nominative singular of short ō-stem nouns}}
#: {{m|ang|ġief'''u'''||gift}}
#: {{m|ang|luf'''u'''||love}}

===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-u}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-uz}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix|head=-u}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the nominative and {{lg|accusative}} singular of short u-stem nouns}}
#: {{m|ang|sun'''u'''||son}}
#: {{m|ang|wud'''u'''||forest}}

===Etymology 5===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*-u}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-ō}}

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix|head=-u|g=n}}

# {{n-g|Used to form the nominative and accusative {{lg|plural}} of short a-stem and all ija-stem neuter nouns}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|clif|gloss1=cliff|clifu|gloss2=cliffs}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|sċip|gloss1=ship|sċipu|gloss2=ships}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|rīċe|gloss1=kingdom|rīċu|gloss2=kingdoms}}

===Etymology 6===

====Suffix====
{{head|ang|suffix}}

# {{n-g|Forms the strong nominative feminine singular and strong nominative/accusative neuter plural of adjectives}}
#: {{suffixusex|ang|unārīmedlīċ|unārīmedlīċu}}

-------- end text --------
