Page 220 -unga: -------- begin text --------
==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|-inga|-enga|-onga|-ningas|-nunges}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|ang|-ung}}, noun suffix + {{m|ang|-a}}, adverbial suffix.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|-un+ga}}

===Suffix===
{{h|ang|suf}}<!--When I get a chance I will look at these senses. I think -unga/-inga is actually added to verbs, with ''hwæthwugununges'' possibly being derived from a ghost verb or by analogy to so-derived adverbs [?] -->

# used to form adverbs from [[noun|nouns]], originally from verbal nouns
#: {{uxi|ang|[[fǣringa]]|suddenly}}
#: {{uxi|ang|[[wēninga]]|possibly, perhaps, by chance}}
#: {{uxi|ang|[[healfunga]]|by halves, partially, to some extent, imperfectly}}
#: {{uxi|ang|[[hōlunga]]|in vain, without reason}}
# used to form adverbs from [[adjective|adjectives]]
#: {{uxi|ang|[[fullunga]]|fully}}
#: {{uxi|ang|[[dearnunga]]|secretly, privately, latently}}
# used to create adverbs from pronouns
#: {{m|ang|hwæthwegununges}}, {{m|ang|hwæthuguningas|t=somewhat|pos=adverb}}
# used to create adverbs from prepositions
#: {{uxi|ang|[[ġēanunga]]|directly}}

====Usage notes====
* ''-unga'' is properly the suffix used with Class 2 Weak verbs, and ''-inga'' with all others. Occasionally the distinction is blurred and forms exist that do not adhere to the original rule.

====Derived terms====
{{suffixsee|ang}}

